## 1. Create
   concept: INSERT the row and an audit entry (before=null, after=row) in one transaction
   created: { id: 1, name: 'Keyboard', price: '49.00' }
   check ok: create writes one audit row: before=null, after=the new row

## 2. Update
   concept: lock the row (SELECT ... FOR UPDATE), capture before, UPDATE, write audit with before and after
   bob: { id: 1, name: 'Keyboard', price: '39.00' }
   alice: { id: 1, name: 'Mech Keyboard', price: '39.00' }
   check ok: each update writes one audit row with the before and after values

## 3. Atomicity
   concept: if the audit insert fails (empty actor violates CHECK), the data change rolls back with it
   rejected: new row for relation "audit_log" violates check constraint "audit_log_actor_check"
   price still: 39.00
   check ok: the update with an empty actor is rejected
   check ok: the rolled-back update left the price unchanged and wrote no audit row

## 4. Delete
   concept: the row disappears from products, but the audit entry keeps its last value (before=row, after=null)
   read after delete: null
   check ok: the row is gone from products after the delete

## 5. History
   concept: the products table only knows the present; audit_log answers who changed what, when, from what to what
   2026-10-04T00:48:31.719Z create by alice before=null after={"id":1,"name":"Keyboard","price":"49.00"}
   2026-10-04T00:48:31.727Z update by bob   before={"id":1,"name":"Keyboard","price":"49.00"} after={"id":1,"name":"Keyboard","price":"39.00"}
   2026-10-04T00:48:31.730Z update by alice before={"id":1,"name":"Keyboard","price":"39.00"} after={"id":1,"name":"Mech Keyboard","price":"39.00"}
   2026-10-04T00:48:31.742Z delete by carol before={"id":1,"name":"Mech Keyboard","price":"39.00"} after=null
   check ok: the history survives the delete: one audit row per change (create, update, update, delete), the last one keeping the final value
   check ok: audit_log holds no row from the rolled-back change

